"Whitney, Hassler - INFORMS (https://www.informs.org/Explore/History-of-O.R.-Excellence/Biographical-Profiles/Whitney-Hassler)\nciteturn880search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; As a geometrician, Whitney made important contributions to singularity theory and topology. ... Whitney Biography. ... Yale University, BA 1928 & 1929 ... Hassler Whitney (1984) Interview by Albert Tucker, April 10. ... New York Times (1989) Hassler Whitney, Geometrician; He Eased 'Mathematics Anxiety'.\n\n# Hassler Whitney\n\nMarch 23, 1907 – May 10, 1989\n\n### Brief Biography\n\nHassler Whitney was born in New York City to State Supreme Court judge Edward B. Whitney and local artist and politician Josepha Newcomb Whitney. The young Whitney attended Yale University, where he received degrees in both physics and music, playing the viola and violin. He went on to Harvard University where he studied under George Birkoff and completed his PhD dissertation on graph theory. As a geometrician, Whitney made important contributions to singularity theory and topology. He spent his first postdoctoral years at Harvard prior to joining the Institute for Advanced Study at Princeton University in 1935.\n\nAmong his other contributions, Whitney is credited as the original source of what would become the travelling salesman problem (TSP). Merrill M. Flood, the Princeton PhD who popularized first it, was introduced to the problem by Albert W. Tucker. Tucker, in turn, traced his knowledge and developments of the problem back to a series of conversations held with Whitney in the early 1930s. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nISSN 0002-9920 (print) (https://www.ams.org/journals/notices/201809/201809FullIssue.pdf)\nciteturn880search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; NEVER A DULL MOMENTHASSLER WHITNEY, MATHEMATICS PIONEERKeith Kendig, Cleveland State University, OH ... This biography is a revealing portrait of a fascinat- ... the April 2018 Notices.\nNotices\nof the American Mathematical Society\nAMS / MAA Press\nNEVER A DULL MOMENT\nHASSLER WHITNEY, MATHEMATICS PIONEER\nKeith Kendig, Cleveland State University, OH\nIN EVERY ISSUE\n1107 T e Back Page \n1118 Mathematics People \n1124 Mathematics Opportunities \n1128 Inside the AMS \n1134 Classiﬁ ed Advertising \n1140 New Publications Oﬀ ered by the AMS \n1149 Meetings and Conferences of the AMS\nCover: National Aquarium, Inner Harbor, Baltimore, \nMaryland, USA. Credit: Glowimages. Courtesy of \nGetty Images. \nEDITOR'S NOTE.\nThis biography is a revealing portrait of a fascinat-\ning personality and a titan of twentieth-century \nmathematics, Hassler Whitney. Whitney is prob-\nably best known for introducing the grandfather \nof today’s innumerable embedding theorems—\nhis strong embedding theorem stating that any \nsmooth manifold can be smoothly embedded in a \nEuclidean space of twice the manifold’s dimension. \nThis in turn led to several standard techniques \nused every day in algebraic topology. Whitney \nalso established the fundamentals of graph the-\nory, the four-color problem, matroids, extending \nsmooth functions, and singularities of smooth \nfunctions. He almost never used complicated tech-\nnical machinery, so most of his work is accessible \nto a general reader with a modest mathematical \nbackground.\nJean-Pierre Bourguignon \nkindly sent us this color ver-\nsion of a photo we ran in “Ad \nHonorem Claire Voisin” in \nthe April 2018 Notices. The \nphoto was taken at the con-\nference at IHÉS for his 60th \nbirthday by Jean-François \nDars.\nSpectrum, Volume 93; 2018; approximately 381 pages; Softcover; \nISBN: 978-1-4704-4828-8; List US$60; AMS Individual member \nUS$45; MAA members US$45; Order code SPEC/93\nThis title is now available for pre-order at\nbookstore.ams.org/spec-93--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCelebratio Mathematica — Whitney — Life &amp; Works (https://celebratio.org/Whitney_H/article/245/)\nciteturn880search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; ### Hassler Whitney: ~~1907–1989~~#### by Keith Kendig ... Hassler Whit­ney (March 23, 1907 – May 10, 1989) was one of the most ori­gin­al and in­flu­en­tial math­em­aticians of the twen­ti­eth cen­tury. ... In the end, his un­usu­al story makes very good sense. ...   8. ... It was all al­gorithms, com­pu­ta­tion, timed tests, …” Such real­iz­a­tions planted the seeds for his last great call­ing and pas­sion in life: chan­ging the way young kids were ex­posed to and taught math.\n\n### Hassler Whitney: ~~1907–1989~~\n\n#### by Keith Kendig\n\nPDF version Published: 1 August 2013\n\nHassler Whit­ney (March 23, 1907 – May 10, 1989) was one of the most ori­gin­al and in­flu­en­tial math­em­aticians of the twen­ti­eth cen­tury. He cre­ated the field of dif­fer­en­tial to­po­logy, and fa­mil­i­ar terms such as co­homo­logy, cobound­ary, cocycle, cup and cap products, co­homo­logy ring, are all his, stem­ming from his found­a­tion­al work in to­po­logy. He gave the defin­i­tion of dif­fer­en­ti­able man­i­fold still used today, and proved that any dif­fer­en­ti­able man­i­fold can be em­bed­ded in Eu­c­lidean space. His two em­bed­ding the­or­ems can jus­ti­fi­ably be called the grand­fath­ers of all em­bed­ding the­or­ems, and were the pre­curs­ors of hun­dreds of dif­fer­ent em­bed­ding the­or­ems seen today in geo­metry and al­gebra.\n\nHow did he do it? Whit­ney’s story is fas­cin­at­ing. His tale flies in the face of nearly everything con­ven­tion­al, and is one likely to sur­prise most people. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHassler Whitney (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hassler_Whitney)\nciteturn880search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; Death Date: 1989-05-10 (YYYY-MM-DD) ... Hassler Whitney (March 23, 1907 – May 10, 1989) was an American mathematician. ... * Hassler Whitney Page - Whitney Research Group (https://web.archive.org/web/20050215212523/http://whitneygen.org/archives/biography/hassler.html)* Interview with Hassler Whitney about his experiences at Princeton (https://web.archive.org/web/20150310074635/http://www.princeton.edu/~mudd/finding_aids/mathoral/pmc43.htm) ... * INFORMS (https://www.informs.org/About-INFORMS/History-and-Traditions/Biographical-Profiles/Whitney-Hassler): Biography of Hassler Whitney from the Institute for Operations Research and the Management Sciences ... Kendig also writes that him apparently being in good health, the physicians attributed the cause of the stroke to the treatments for prostate cancer he was undergoing. ... Whitney attended Yale University, where he received baccalaureate degrees in physics and in music, respectively in 1928 and in 1929. ... In his book Geometric Integration Theory he gives a theoretical basis for Stokes' theorem applied with singularities on the boundary.\nHassler Whitney\nAmerican mathematician (1907–1989)\n\nName: Hassler Whitney\nCaption: Whitney in 1973\nBirth Date: 1907-03-23 (YYYY-MM-DD)\nBirth Place: New York City, U.S.\nDeath Date: 1989-05-10 (YYYY-MM-DD)\nDeath Place: Princeton, New Jersey, U.S.\nField: Mathematics\nWork Institutions: Harvard University, Institute for Advanced Study, Princeton University, National Science Foundation, National Defense Research Committee\nAlma Mater: Yale University (BA, BM)\nHarvard University (PhD)\nThesis Title: The Coloring of Graphs\nThesis Year: 1932\nThesis Url: https://search.worldcat.org/title/77002000?oclcNum=77002000\nDoctoral Advisor: George David Birkhoff\nDoctoral Students: R. Eliot Chamberlin, James Eells, Wilfred Kaplan, Paul Olum, Herbert Robbins\nKnown For: Algebraic topology, Differential topology, Graph theory, Geometric measure theory, Cohomology, Matroid theory, Singularity theory\nPrizes: National Medal of Science (1976), Wolf Prize (1982), Leroy P. Steele Prize (1985)\nRelatives: Dwight family\n\nHassler Whitney (March 23, 1907 – May 10, 1989) was an American mathematician. The New York Times. May 12, 1989.†www.nytimes.com】.\n* Kendig, Keith. Hassler Whitney. Celebratio Mathematica. August 2013.\n* Thom, René. La vie et l'œuvre de Hassler Whitney. Comptes rendus de l'Académie des sciences. Série générale, La Vie des sciences., available from Gallica.\n* Whitney Hassler. A Century of Mathematics in America, Part I.\n\n### Scientific references\n\n* Chirka, Evgeniǐ Mikhaǐlovich. Complex analytic sets. Kluwer Academic Publishers..\n* Epstein, Marcelo. Whence the Boundary Conditions in Modern Continuum Physics? (Roma 14-16 ottobre 2002). Accademia Nazionale dei Lincei..\n* Federer, Herbert. Review: Geometric integration theory, by H. Whitney. Bulletin of the American Mathematical Society..\n* Steve Nadis, Shing-Tung Yau. A History in Sum. Harvard University Press. (e-book: ).\n* Oxley, James. Matroid Theory. The Clarendon Press, Oxford University Press..\n* Shields, Allen. Differentiable manifolds: Weyl and Whitney. The Mathematical Intelligencer..\n\n\n## External links\n\n* \n* Hassler Whitney Page - Whitney Research Group (https://web.archive.org/web/20050215212523/http://whitneygen.org/archives/biography/hassler.html)\n* Interview with Hassler Whitney about his experiences at Princeton (https://web.archive.org/web/20150310074635/http://www.princeton.edu/~mudd/finding_aids/mathoral/pmc43.htm)\n* Hassler Whitney — The First Century of the International Commission on Mathematical Instruction (http://www.icmihistory.unito.it/portrait/whitney.php)\n* INFORMS (https://www.informs.org/About-INFORMS/History-and-Traditions/Biographical-Profiles/Whitney-Hassler): Biography of Hassler Whitney from the Institute for Operations Research and the Management Sciences\n\nHe would run outside, 6 to 12 miles every other day. As an undergraduate, with his cousin Bradley Gilman, Whitney made the first ascent of the Whitney–Gilman ridge on Cannon Mountain, New Hampshire in 1929. It was the hardest and most famous rock climb in the East. He was a member of the Swiss Alpine Society and the Yale Mountaineering Society (the precursor to the Yale Outdoors Club) and climbed most of the mountain peaks in Switzerland.\n\n- and .\n- \n- According to and : Thom cites Josiah Whitney explicitly while Chern simply states that:-\"... a great uncle was the first to survey Mount Whitney\".\n\n#### Death\n\nThree years after his third marriage, on 10 May 1989, Whitney died in Princeton, after suffering a stroke. In accordance with his wish, Hassler Whitney's ashes rest atop mountain Dent Blanche in Switzerland where Oscar Burlet, another mathematician and member of the Swiss Alpine Club, placed them on August 20, 1989.\n\n- clarifies Princeton, NJ as his correct death place.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe First Century of the International Commission on Mathematical Instruction (1908-2008) - History of ICMI (https://www.icmihistory.unito.it/portrait/whitney.php)\nciteturn880search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; # HAssLER WHITNEY ...   * Brief scientific biography ... Whitney received bachelor's degrees in philosophy (1928) and music (1929) from Yale University. ... In 1952, Whitney joined the Institute of Advanced Study at Princeton as professor of mathematics, officially retiring in 1977 but continuing as an emeritus professor until his death on 10 May 1989 in Princeton, New Jersey, two weeks after suffering a stroke. ... His first research, a byproduct of his dissertation, was in graph theory; he introduced the notion of duality, which he used to characterize planar graphs (1933), and developed a theory of linear dependence that led to the theory of matroids (1935).\n\n# HAssLER WHITNEY\n\n#### New York, New York 1907 - Princeton, New Jersey 1989\n\nImage: whitney\n\n  * Brief scientific biography\n  * Contributions to Education\n  * Relevant bibliography\n  * Publications on the teaching of mathematics\n\n### Brief scientific biography\n\nImage: Whitney-Gilman Ridge\n\nWhitney-Gilman Ridge\n\nHassler Whitney was born 23 March 1907 in New York City. Both of his grandfathers, the philologist William D. Whitney and the astronomer Simon Newcomb, were members of the U.S. National Academy of Sciences, to which Whitney was elected in 1945. Whitney received bachelor's degrees in philosophy (1928) and music (1929) from Yale University. A keen mountaineer all his life, he made a famous climb while still an undergraduate, with his cousin Bradley Gilman, of a cliff in New Hampshire. The cliff was later named the Whitney-Gilman Ridge.\n\nImage: Harvard Yard gate\n\nHarvard Yard gate\n\nFor graduate studies in mathematics, Whitney went to Harvard University, where he earned his Ph.D. under George David Birkhoff in 1932 with a dissertation entitled The Coloring of Graphs, in which he found an equivalent graph-theoretic formulation of the four color problem. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHassler Whitney | Scholars | Institute for Advanced Study (https://www.ias.edu/scholars/hassler-whitney)\nciteturn880search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; From the New York Times, May 12, 1989:Hassler Whitney [was] a mathematician who made important contributions in geometry and . . . was deeply involved in improving the teaching of mathematics. . . . ... Vice President, AMS 1948–50\n\nImage: Hassler Whitney headshot\n\n# Hassler Whitney\n\nPast Faculty\n\nSchool of Mathematics\n\n## Affiliation\n\nMathematics\n\nFrom the New York Times, May 12, 1989:\n\nHassler Whitney [was] a mathematician who made important contributions in geometry and . . . was deeply involved in improving the teaching of mathematics. . . . Whitney's specialty was geometry, particularly topology, including the study of multidimensional surfaces like spheres. He demonstrated, for example, that it is possible to plot a tour of the earth that visits each country once and only once - the Hamiltonian cycle.\n\n## Visits\n\nEmeritus\n\nSchool of Mathematics\n\n7/1977 – 5/1989\n\nFaculty\n\nSchool of Mathematics\n\n9/1952 – 6/1977\n\n## Degrees\n\nHarvard University\n\nPh.D.\n\n1932\n\n## Honors\n\n1947\n\nHonorary D.Sc., Yale University\n\n1946\n\nAMS Colloquium Lecturer\n\nNational Medal of Science 1977 Appointments: Member Math Panel, National Defense Res Comm, 1943–45\n\nPresident, International Committee on Math Instruction, 1979–82\n\nVice President, AMS 1948–50--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHassler Whitney (1907 - 1989) - Biography - MacTutor History of Mathematics (https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk//Biographies/Whitney/)\nciteturn880search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.1 years ago;     Hassler Whitney was an American mathematician who did important work in manifolds, embeddings, immersions, characteristic classes and geometric integration theory. ... John McCleary writes in his article Hassler Whitney, the Applied Mathematics Panel, and Airborne Weapons Accuracy:- ... Following this he published a number of papers on graph theory such as A theorem on graphs (1931), Non-separable and planar graphs (1932), Congruent graphs and the connectivity of graphs (1932), The coloring of graphs (1932), A numerical equivalent of the four color map problem (1937). ... Intelligencer 10 (2) (1988), 5-9. ... J D Zund, Hassler Whitney, American National Biography 23 (Oxford, 1999), 303-304.\n\n# Hassler Whitney\n\n* * *\n\n### Quick Info\n\nBorn\n    23 March 1907\nNew York, USA\nDied\n    10 May 1989\nMount Dents Blanches, Switzerland\n\n* * *\n\nSummary\n    Hassler Whitney was an American mathematician who did important work in manifolds, embeddings, immersions, characteristic classes and geometric integration theory.\n\n### Biography\n\nHassler Whitney's father was Edward Baldwin Whitney, a judge, and his mother was A Josepha Newcomb. Edward's father was William Dwight Whitney who was a linguist and one of the foremost Sanskrit scholars of his time, noted especially for his classic work, Sanskrit Grammar (1879). Josepha's father was Simon Newcomb who has a biography in this archive. Certainly Hassler had two very famous grandfathers.\nWhitney attended Yale University where he received his first degree in 1928, then continued to undertake mathematical research at Harvard University from where his doctorate was awarded in 1932. His doctorate was awarded for a dissertation The Coloring of Graphs written under Birkhoff's supervision. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNEWSLETTER\nIssue: 479 - November 2018\nTHE STATISTI (https://www.lms.ac.uk/sites/lms.ac.uk/files/files/NLMS_479_for%20web.pdf)\nciteturn880search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Dec 2018 389pp 9781470446963 Hardback £71.95 ... Mathematician and award-winning children's book author Richard Schwartz teaches about infinity and ... NEVER A DULL MOMENTHassler Whitney, Mathematics Pioneer ... This biography paints a picture of him and ... close friendship and many conversations over decades, author Kendig was able to see how he actually worked. ... treated in sufficient depth to enable the reader to pursue research in representation theory.\nNEVER A DULL MOMENT\nHassler Whitney, Mathematics Pioneer \nKeith Kendig, Cleveland State University\nHassler Whitney was a giant of twentieth-century mathematics. This biography paints a picture of him and \nincludes dozens of revealing anecdotes. Mathematically, he had a rare detector that went off whenever he \nspotted a piece of mathematical gold, and he would then draw countless pictures, gradually forging a path \nfrom hunch to proof. This geometric path is seldom reflected in the rigor of his formal papers, but thanks to a \nclose friendship and many conversations over decades, author Kendig was able to see how he actually worked. \nSpectrum, Vol. 93 \nMAA Press\nNov 2018 381pp 9781470448288 Paperback £57.50\nA TOUR OF REPRESENTATION THEORY\nMartin Lorenz, Temple University\nOffers an introduction to four different flavours of representation theory: representations of algebras, groups, \nLie algebras, and Hopf algebras. A separate part of the book is devoted to each of these areas and they are all \ntreated in sufficient depth to enable the reader to pursue research in representation theory.\nGraduate Studies in Mathematics, Vol. 193\nOct 2018 654pp 9781470436803 Hardback £90.50 \nFree delivery at eurospanbookstore.com/ams\nAMS is distributed by Eurospan|group\nCUSTOMER SERVICES:\nTel: +44 (0)1767 604972\nFax: +44 (0)1767 601640\nEmail: eurospan@turpin-distribution.com\nFURTHER INFORMATION:\nTel: +44 (0)20 7240 0856\nFax: +44 (0)20 7379 0609\nEmail: info@eurospangroup.com--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNever a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer: Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer by Keith Kendig, pp. 385, £58.50 (paper), ISBN 978-1-4704-4828-8, MAA Press, American Mathematical Society (2018).: The Mathematical Gazette: Vol 103, No 558 (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1017/mag.2019.132)\nciteturn880search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer## Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer by Keith Kendig, pp.385, £58.50 (paper), ISBN 978-1-4704-4828-8, MAA Press, American Mathematical Society (2018).\n\nThe Mathematical Gazette Volume 103, 2019 - Issue 558\n\nSubmit an article Journal homepage\n\n0\n\nViews\n\n0\n\nCrossRef citations to date\n\n0\n\nAltmetric\n\nReviews\n\n# Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer\n\n## Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer by Keith Kendig, pp. 385, £58.50 (paper), ISBN 978-1-4704-4828-8, MAA Press, American Mathematical Society (2018).\n\nPeter GiblinDepartment of Mathematical Sciences, The University of Liverpool e-mail: pjgiblin@liv.ac.uk\n\nPage 571 | Published online: 19 Jan 2026\n\n  * Cite this article\n  * https://doi.org/10.1017/mag.2019.132\n  * CrossMark\n\n  * Citations\n  * Metrics\n  * Reprints & Permissions\n  * Read this article /doi/epdf/10.1017/mag.2019.132?needAccess=true\n\n\"Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer.\" The Mathematical Gazette, 103(558), p. 571\n\nPlease note: Selecting permissions does not provide access to the full text of the article,\nplease see our help page How do I view content?\n\n## To cite this article:\n\nReference style: [Input]APA [Input]Chicago [Input]Harvard\n\nCitation copied to clipboard\n\nCopy citation to clipboard\n\nReference styles above use APA (6th edition), Chicago (16th edition) & Harvard (10th edition)\n\n### Download citation\n\nDownload a citation file in RIS format that can be imported by citation management software including EndNote, ProCite, RefWorks and Reference Manager.\n\nChoose format: [Input]RIS [Input]BibTex [Input]RefWorks Direct Export\n\nChoose options: [Input]Citation [Input]Citation & abstract [Input]Citation & references--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJosepha Newcomb Whitney (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josepha_Newcomb_Whitney)\nciteturn880search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; - Keith Kendig, Never a Dull Moment: Hassler Whitney, Mathematics Pioneer (American Mathematical Society 2018): 11. ... Her son Hassler Whitney (1907–1989) was a noted mathematician.\nThe Newcomb daughters were mainly educated at home by their father. Josepha Newcomb later joined classes with the Art Students League of New York.\n\n- Daughters of the American Revolution, Lineage Book, Volume 4 (1897): 6.\n- Keith Kendig, Never a Dull Moment: Hassler Whitney, Mathematics Pioneer (American Mathematical Society 2018): 11.\n- Andrew Newcomb, 1618–1686, and His Descendants, Volume 1 (Tuttle, Morehouse & Taylor Company 1923): 542.\n\n## Activism\n\nWhitney was a prominent suffragist in Connecticut and New York. In 1912, Whitney planned the first suffrage meeting in Cornwall, Connecticut. She was president of the local Equal Suffrage League and the Housewives' League. \"Where dirt is,\" she said of corruption in politics, \"it is the custom of woman to get her broom and clean it up.\" During World War I, she worked on the assembly line at a munitions plant in Winchester, Connecticut, to study the working conditions of women in the war effort. She also conducted a food price survey.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer by Keith Kendig, pp. 385, £58.50 (paper), ISBN 978-1-4704-4828-8, MAA Press, American Mathematical Society (2018). (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/336701255_Never_a_dull_moment_Hassler_Whitney_mathematics_pioneer_by_Keith_Kendig_pp_385_5850_paper_ISBN_978-1-4704-4828-8_MAA_Press_American_Mathematical_Society_2018)\nciteturn880search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Never a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer by Keith Kendig, pp.385, £58.50 (paper), ISBN 978-1-4704-4828-8, MAA Press, American Mathematical Society (2018). ... Hassler Whitney (1907-1989), ... This unusual mathematical biography by Keith Kendig, a close friend of Whitney’s from\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nNever a dull moment: Hassler Whitney, mathematics pioneer by Keith Kendig, pp.385,\n\n£58.50 (paper), ISBN 978-1-4704-4828-8, MAA Press, American Mathematical Society\n\n(2018).\n\nAccording to Freeman Dyson, G.H.Hardy, referring at the time to himself, said that ‘Young\n\nmen should prove theorems, old men should write books’. Hassler Whitney (1907-1989),\n\nwhose influence on the mathematics of his time and ours is very great, spent his older years\n\nnot writing books but trying to improve the mathematical education of children in the United\n\nStates. His approach to early years teaching is roughly the ‘discovery’ or ‘problem-solving’\n\napproach.\n\nThis unusual mathematical biography by Keith Kendig, a close friend of Whitney’s from\n\n1965 and author of several books on alg ebraic geometry, takes the reader through Whitney’s\n\nmany groundbreaking mathematical achievements and his later work with schools. There is\n\nenough detail of his ancestry (which includes the astronomer Simon Newcomb), and his\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArchive:Hassler Whitney (1907-1989) - WRG (https://whitneygen.org/wrg/index.php?title=Archive%3AHassler_Whitney_%281907-1989%29)\nciteturn880search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.3 years ago;     WHITNEY, HASSLER, mathematician; b. ... 8, 1986. ...     For a mathematical biography, see the one at the MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive at St. ...     Hassler Whitney (23 March 1907 - 10 May 1989) was an American mathematician, who was one of the founders of singularity theory. ... D., Josiah Dwight^{7}, Abel^{6}, Aaron^{5}, Moses^{4}, Moses^{3}, Richard^{2}, John^{1},\nMath. Instrn., 1979-82; research math. Nat. Def. Research Com., 1943-45; cons. sch. math. Recipient Nat. Medal of Sci., 1976, Wolf prize Wolf Found., 1983. Mem. NAS, Am. Math. Soc. (Colloquium lectr. 1946, v.p. 1948-50, editor Am. Jour. Math., 1944-49, editor Math. Revs. 1949-54, chmn. com. vis. lectureship, 1946-51, com. summer insts. 1953-54, Steel prize 1985), Math. Assn. Am., Nat. Council Tchrs. Math., Swiss Math. Soc. (hon.), Académie des Scis. (fgn. assoc.), N.Y. Acad. of Scis. Club: Am. Alpine (N.Y.C.). Home: Princeton, N.J. Died May 10, 1989; buried Mont Dents Blanches, Switzerland.\n\n    For a mathematical biography, see the one at the MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive at St. Andrews University, Scotland. His collected papers have been published, and can be purchased on-line at Amazon.com.\n\n    In addition to an